    {
      "id": "cmtj7pxht000105l4xxtnhlbk",
      "term": "Why do LBOs use leverage? How do they use leverage to increase returns?",
      "setTitle": "LBO",
      "klps": [
        {
          "text": "Leverage in an LBO means funding the purchase price largely with borrowed debt instead of equity",
          "kind": "definition"
        },
        {
          "text": "Debt reduces the upfront equity the sponsor must commit for a given purchase price",
          "kind": "mechanism"
        },
        {
          "text": "Sponsor returns are measured as equity value at exit versus equity invested at entry, so a smaller equity base makes the same dollar gain a larger percentage return",
          "kind": "mechanism"
        },
        {
          "text": "Each dollar of debt principal paid down converts creditor claims into equity value, so the sponsor's ownership of the business grows over the hold",
          "kind": "causal"
        },
        {
          "text": "Combining a smaller initial equity check with debt paydown and value growth is how leverage amplifies the sponsor's return — the answer to why LBOs use it",
          "kind": "causal"
        }
      ],
