    {
      "id": "cmtjcmjdb000j04k42m4m2u0k",
      "term": "How long will a P/E firm generally hold on to a company? What is the typical exit strategy?",
      "setTitle": "LBO",
      "klps": [
        {
          "text": "PE firms generally hold a company for about 3 to 7 years before exiting to realize their gain.",
          "kind": "definition"
        },
        {
          "text": "The 3-to-7-year window is long enough to execute operational improvements and pay down debt.",
          "kind": "mechanism"
        },
        {
          "text": "It is short enough to return capital to LPs within a roughly ten-year fund life.",
          "kind": "condition"
        },
        {
          "text": "The first exit route is a sale to another PE firm — a secondary or sponsor-to-sponsor deal.",
          "kind": "example"
        },
        {
          "text": "The second route is a sale to a strategic buyer, who may pay a premium because it can capture synergies the PE firm cannot.",
          "kind": "example"
        },
        {
